<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/qldoctor-header-rb.7542.jpg" width="800" height="226" style="margin:0 auto;clear:both;" class="header" /></p>
<p>Press Spacebar on a Markdown file and macOS will now draw <em>something</em>. The latest system ships its own Markdown Quick Look plugin, so you get a tidy little preview instead of a wall of monospace. It is fine. It is also the preview equivalent of a plain cracker. If the note has a diagram, a table of contents, a house style, or one of those Markdown dialects you swore you would stop using, you are still looking at a sketch of the document.</p>

<p><a href="https://markedapp.com/ql/">Marked QL</a> is the Spacebar preview I actually want open. It is a small app whose only job is to render Markdown the way Marked does, inside Quick Look.</p>

<h3 id="a-table-of-contents-right-there">A table of contents, right there</h3>

<p>Long notes are miserable in a preview that only scrolls. Marked QL builds a table of contents from the headings and puts it beside the page. Two or more headings and it shows up. Drag the divider, collapse it, jump to a section. Finder&rsquo;s narrow column preview hides it on its own when there is no room, so a glance at a file in column view stays a glance.</p>

<h3 id="diagrams-and-colored-code">Diagrams and colored code</h3>

<p>A fenced block that starts with <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mermaid</code> is a diagram, not a code sample you are supposed to squint at.</p>

<div class="language-markdown highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight fixed"><code><span class="p">```</span><span class="nl">mermaid
</span><span class="sb">graph LR
  A[Spacebar] --&gt; B[Marked QL]
  B --&gt; C[The actual note]</span>
<span class="p">```</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>That renders in the preview. So does syntax highlighting, with a choice of themes, and math. The scripts ship inside the app. The preview does not phone home to draw a flowchart.</p>

<h3 id="styles-that-are-actually-yours">Styles that are actually yours</h3>

<p>Nine Marked styles are built in: GitHub, Swiss, Ink, Manuscript, Grump, and the rest. Pick one in Settings and the next Spacebar uses it. If you already have a stylesheet, import it. Custom CSS is the difference between &ldquo;a preview of my notes&rdquo; and &ldquo;a preview that looks like my notes.&rdquo;</p>

<h3 id="quarto-and-the-weird-markdown">Quarto, and the weird Markdown</h3>

<p>Most of my files are ordinary Markdown. Some of them are not. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.qmd</code> files can use Apex&rsquo;s Quarto mode, which covers the Pandoc and Quarto bits you need for an HTML preview. It is not a full Quarto render. It is enough to read the document without exporting it first.</p>

<p>Under that is <a href="https://github.com/ApexMarkdown/apex">Apex</a>, the processor Marked QL uses for everything. Unified mode is the default, and it is the one that swallows the dialects people actually mix together. You can switch the preview to MultiMarkdown, GitHub Flavored Markdown, CommonMark, or Kramdown when a file is picky. CriticMarkup shows up as tracked changes. The odd flavor is still a document, not a syntax error.</p>

<h3 id="when-apples-preview-wins-anyway">When Apple&rsquo;s preview wins anyway</h3>

<p>Installing Marked QL is not always the end of the story. macOS decides which Quick Look extension draws the file, and it is happy to keep using its own.</p>

<p>Here is the annoying version. You install the app, you turn the extension on, you press Spacebar, and you get Apple&rsquo;s plain Markdown preview. Or raw source. Marked QL can be registered and enabled while the system plugin still handles <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.md</code>. Recent macOS tags Markdown with its own types (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">public.markdown</code>, and on newer systems <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">public.utf8-plain-text</code>), and the built-in preview claims those. Other apps do the same thing. Folder Quick Look, a notes app, an editor. Whoever claimed the type last, or loudest, gets the Spacebar.</p>

<p>Preferring your plugin in System Settings is supposed to fix this. Often it does not. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">pluginkit</code> will show Marked QL with a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">+</code> and the preview on screen will still be someone else&rsquo;s. I have stared at that output long enough to stop pretending the Settings checkbox is the whole tool.</p>

<p>That is what <a href="https://markedapp.com/ql/doctor/">Quick Look Doctor</a> is for. It is free, it is not just for Markdown, and it does not ask you to memorize Terminal flags.</p>

<p>Drop the file that is previewing wrong. Doctor lists every extension that claims it and shows which one is actually serving the preview. Prefer Marked QL. It will ignore the others that are in the way, ask first, and let you undo. There is also a list of every Quick Look plugin on the machine if you want to see the whole crowd: Use, Ignore, or back to Default.</p>

<p>A couple of honest limits. Doctor does not tattoo Marked QL onto the file forever. macOS can still change its mind after an OS update, and another app can register itself again the next time it launches. You run Doctor, you point the preview at the handler you want, you get on with the note. If you would rather read the long version first, the <a href="https://markedapp.com/ql/troubleshooting/">troubleshooting page</a> has the manual steps Doctor is saving you from.</p>

<h3 id="get-the-preview-you-meant-to-install">Get the preview you meant to install</h3>

<p><a href="https://markedapp.com/ql/">Marked QL</a> is the preview. <a href="https://markedapp.com/ql/doctor/">Quick Look Doctor</a> is how you get macOS to use it when Apple&rsquo;s own plugin, or anyone else&rsquo;s, has stepped in front. Spacebar should show the document you wrote, diagrams and all.</p>

